-> What is a searching algorithm? Why is it important?

A searching algorithm is a method for finding an element in a data structure (array, list, tree, etc.). It
is important for retrieving, modifying, or deleting data efficiently.

There are the two main types of searching algorithms
a) Linear Search (Sequential Search) : O(n)
b) Binary Search (Divide & Conquer) : O(log n)

Linear Search:
Check each element one by one until you find the target. This work on any data structure where you can traverse elements sequentially:
Array, Vector, Linked list, BST, Genric Tree
Time complexity: O(N), because you may have to check every node
Space Complexcity: O(1)

Binary Search:
Keep dividing the array in half to find the target. Works only on sorted arrays.
Array, vector, BST (follow left/right), linked list ❌ (inefficient), genric Tree ❌ (not sorted)
Time complexity: O(log N) on a balanced BST, O(N) on a skewed BST
Space Complexcity: O(1)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-> When should you use Linear Search over Binary Search?

: Linear Search
When the dataset is small-
When the data is unsorted and one-time search is needed (sorting costs extra time)
When the dataset is dynamic (frequent insertions/deletions)
Binary Search better than Linear Search for large datasets
