-> What is a sorting algorithm? What are the types of sorting algorithms?

A sorting algorithm is a method used to arrange elements in a specific order (ascending or
descending). Sorting is important because:
• It helps in searching (Binary Search needs sorted data).
• It improves data readability and organization.
• It is widely used in database indexing, scheduling, and optimization.

Sorting algorithms are classified into:
1. Comparison-based Sorting (O(n log n) or O(n²))
   : Bubble Sort, Selection Sort, Insertion Sort, Merge Sort, Quick Sort, Heap Sort.
2. Non-Comparison-based Sorting (O(n))
   : Counting Sort, Radix Sort, Bucket Sort.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-> What is the difference between stable and unstable sorting algorithms? 
-> difference between in-place and out-of-place sorting?

Stable Sorting: Maintains the relative order of duplicate elements.
Unstable Sorting: Does not maintain the relative order of duplicate elements.

Algorithm Example
Bubble Sort Yes [2,1,2',3] → [1,2,2',3]
Merge Sort Yes [4,4',2,1] → [1,2,4,4']
Quick Sort No [5,3,5',1] → [1,3,5',5]
Heap Sort No [9,2,2',5] → [2',2,5,9]

In-place Sorting: Uses constant or O(1) extra space (e.g., Quick Sort, Bubble Sort).
Out-of-place Sorting: Requires extra memory (O(n)) (e.g., Merge Sort, Counting Sort).
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

->  Which sorting algorithms are best for Small data sets?

Insertion Sort (O(n²)) (for nearly sorted small arrays).
Selection Sort (O(n²)) (if swapping cost is low).
Bubble Sort (O(n²)) (only when stability is needed).

-> Which sorting algorithms are best for Large datasets?

Merge Sort (O(n log n)) (if stability is needed).
Quick Sort (O(n log n)) (if in-place sorting is needed).
Heap Sort (O(n log n)) (if guaranteed performance is needed).

